          %h2#definition Definition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong distributive lattice
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
              in which
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/join"} join
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∨
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vee
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meet"} meet
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∧
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
              %em distribute
              over each other, in that for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y,z
              in the lattice, the
              %em distributivity laws
              are satisfied:
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee (y \wedge z) = (x \vee y) \wedge (x \vee z)
                ,
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge (y \vee z) = (x \wedge y) \vee (x \wedge z)
                \.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p The nullary forms of distributivity hold in any lattice:
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo ⊤
                      %mo =
                      %mo ⊤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee \top = \top
                ,
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mo =
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge \bot = \bot
                \.
            %p
              Distributive lattices and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice"} lattice
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
              form a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+category"} concrete category
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/DistLat"} DistLat
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Any lattice that satisfies one of the two binary distributivity laws must also satisfy the other; isn't that nice? (This may safely be left as an
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exercise"} exercise
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+category"} distributive category
              (when viewed as a
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+category"} thin category
            %p
              This convenience does
              %em not
              extend to infinitary distributivity, however.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p Each distributivity law holds in one direction in any lattice (the proofs of which may be left as an exercise):
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee (y \wedge z) \leq (x \vee y) \wedge (x \vee z)
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge (y \vee z) \geq (x \wedge y) \vee (x \wedge z)
            %p As a result, the real content of the definition consists of the converse directions (and as remarked above, either one suffices):
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≥
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee (y \wedge z) \geq (x \vee y) \wedge (x \vee z)
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge (y \vee z) \leq (x \wedge y) \vee (x \wedge z)
          %h2#alternative_characterizations Alternative characterizations
          %p
            As mentioned above, the theory of distributive lattices is self-dual, something that is proved in almost any account (or left as an exercise), but which is not manifestly obvious from the standard definition which chooses one of the two distributivity laws and goes from there. In this section we provide some other characterizations or axiomatizations which
            %em are
            manifestly self-dual.
          %p Here is one such characterization:
          #sd1.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p A lattice is distributive if and only if the identity
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a \wedge b) \vee (a \wedge c) \vee (b \wedge c) = (a \vee b) \wedge (a \vee c) \wedge (b \vee c)
            %p is satisfied.
          %p Again this may be left as a (somewhat mechanical) exercise.
          %p
            Perhaps more useful in practice is the characterization in terms of “forbidden sublattices” due to Birkhoff. Namely, introduce the “pentagon”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_5
            as the 5-element lattice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\bot \leq a, b, c \leq \top\}
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \leq c
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            is incomparable to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b, c
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot = a \wedge c
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mo ⊤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee b = \top
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_3
            as the 5-element lattice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ≤
                  %mo ⊤
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\bot \leq a', b', c' \leq \top\}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a', b', c'
            pairwise incomparable. Both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_5
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_3
            are self-dual. Birkhoff’s characterization is the following (manifestly self-dual) criterion.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              A lattice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is distributive if and only if there is no
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+function"} embedding
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_5
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_3
              into
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              that preserves binary meets and binary joins.
          %p
            This can be useful for determining distributivity or its failure, especially in cases where one can visualize a lattice via its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hasse+diagram"} Hasse diagram
          %p
            The necessity of the forbidden sublattice condition is clear in view of the fact that the cancellation law stated in the next result fails in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_5
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_3
          #sd2.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              A lattice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is distributive if and only if the cancellation law holds: for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y, z \in L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = z
              whenever
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge y = x \wedge z
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee y = x \vee z
              (for some
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              “Only if”: if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge y = x \wedge z
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee y = x \vee z
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = y \vee (x \wedge y) = y \vee (x \wedge z) = (y \vee x) \wedge (y \vee z) = (x \vee z) \wedge (y \vee z) = (x \wedge y) \vee z
            %p
              which implies
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi z
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \leq y
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \leq z
            %p
              “If”: this is harder. Assuming the cancellation law for
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is modular. Recall from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+lattice"} modular lattice
              that for any lattice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b \in L
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+connection"} Galois connection
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi x
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo :
                    %mi y
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ([a \wedge b, b] \to [a, a \vee b]: x \mapsto a \vee x) \; \dashv \; ([a, a \vee b] \to [a \wedge b, b]: y \mapsto b \wedge y)
            %p
              and that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is
              %em modular
              if this Galois connection is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Galois+correspondence"} Galois correspondence
              (or
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+equivalence"} adjoint equivalence
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in [a \wedge b, b]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \vee x = a \vee (b \wedge (a \vee x))
              because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mi g
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = f g f
              for any Galois connection
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ⊣
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \dashv g
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi x
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \wedge b \leq x \leq b
              we also have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \wedge x = a \wedge b \wedge x = a \wedge b = a \wedge b \wedge (a \vee x)
            %p
              and so by cancellation of the
              = succeed "'s," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = b \wedge (a \vee x)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in [a, a \vee b]
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∨
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = a \vee (b \wedge y)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is modular.
            %p
              Now we show
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is distributive. Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y, z \in L
              and consider the three elements
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi u
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≔
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi v
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≔
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi w
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≔
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi z
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi z
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    u &amp; \coloneqq &amp; [x \wedge (y \vee z)] \vee (y \wedge z) &amp; = &amp; [x \vee (y \wedge z)] \wedge (y \vee z) \\
                    v &amp; \coloneqq &amp; [y \wedge (x \vee z)] \vee (x \wedge z) &amp; = &amp; [y \vee (x \wedge z)] \wedge (x \vee z) \\
                    w &amp; \coloneqq &amp; [z \wedge (x \vee y)] \vee (x \wedge y) &amp; = &amp; [z \vee (x \wedge y)] \wedge (x \vee y)
                    }
            %p where the non-definitional equalities follow from modularity. Using the first expressions, we compute
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi u
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi v
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ∨
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi y
                            %mo ∨
                            %mi z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    u \vee v &amp; = &amp; [x \wedge (y \vee z)] \vee (y \wedge z) \vee [y \wedge (x \vee z)] \vee (x \wedge z) \\
                    \&amp; = &amp; [x \wedge (y \vee z)] \vee [y \wedge (x \vee z)] \\
                    \&amp; = &amp; [(x \wedge (y \vee z)) \vee y] \wedge (x \vee z) \\
                    \&amp; = &amp; (x \vee y) \wedge (x \vee z) \wedge (y \vee z)
                    }
            %p
              where the third and fourth lines use modularity. By symmetry in the letters
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y, z
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi w
                      %mo =
                      %mi v
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi w
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \vee w = v \vee w = (x \vee y) \wedge (x \vee z) \wedge (y \vee z)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi v
                    %mo =
                    %mi u
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi w
                    %mo =
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi w
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u \wedge v = u \wedge w = v \wedge w = (x \wedge y) \vee (x \wedge z) \vee (y \wedge z).
            %p
              Now by cancellation of the
              = succeed "'s," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo =
                      %mi w
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v = w
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi w
                    %mo =
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi w
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∨
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \vee y) \wedge (x \vee z) \wedge (y \vee z) = v \vee w = v \wedge w = (x \wedge y) \vee (x \wedge z) \vee (y \wedge z)
            %p
              so that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              is distributive by Proposition
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#sd1"}
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              While the expressions for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi w
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u, v, w
              in the preceding proof may look as though they come out of thin air, the underlying idea is that the sublattice of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              generated by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y, z
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
              of a lattice map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(3) \to L
              out of the free modular lattice
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(3)
              on three elements. The only obstruction to distributivity in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(3)
              is the presence of an
              = succeed "-sublattice" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi M
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_3
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2015/09/the_free_modular_lattice_on_3.html#c049685"} Hasse diagram
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo ,
                    %mi v
                    %mo ,
                    %mi w
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u, v, w
              given above, and the proof shows that under the cancellation law, we have
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo =
                    %mi v
                    %mo =
                    %mi w
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u = v = w
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              and removing the obstruction to distributivity.
          %p
            From Proposition
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#sd2"}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi M
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_3
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_5
            in a non-distributive lattice
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is deduced from a failure of the cancellation law where we have three elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y, z
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \wedge y = x \wedge z
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \vee y = x \vee z
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≠
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \neq z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y, z
            are comparable, say
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \leq z
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x \wedge y, x, y, z, x \vee y\}
            forms an
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_5
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y, z
            are incomparable, then we have either
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi z
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∨
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \vee z \lt x \vee y
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \wedge z \gt x \wedge y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \vee z = x \vee y
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \wedge z = x \wedge y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_5
            (e.g.,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x \wedge y, x, y, y \vee z, x \vee y\}
            for the first case), and in the third case the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x \wedge y, x, y, z, x \vee y\}
            forms an
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_3
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            and every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-frame"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              \-frame
            is a distributive lattice.
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+total+order"} bounded total order
            is a distributive lattice.
          %p
            Any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+order"} linear order
            is a distributive lattice.
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+domain"} integral domain
            is a
            %span.newWikiWord
              Prüfer domain
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Pr%C3%BCfer+domain"} ?
            iff its lattice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideals"} ideals
            is distributive. The classical example is
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            ordered by divisibility, with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            at the bottom and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            at the top.
          %p
            The lattice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Young+diagrams"} Young diagrams
            ordered by inclusion is distributive.
          %h2#infinitely_distributive_property Infinitely distributive property
          %p
            A distributive lattice that is complete (not necessarily
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completely+distributive+lattice"} completely distributive
            %strong infinitely distributive
            or said to satisfiy the
            %strong infinite distributive law
            \:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \wedge (\bigvee_i y_i) = \bigvee_i (x\wedge y_i)
          %p
            This property is sufficient to give the lattice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+algebra"} Heyting algebra
            stucture where the implication
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\Rightarrow b
            (or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponential object
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi b
                      %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b^a
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi u
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi v
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (u \Rightarrow v) = \bigvee_{x \wedge u \leq v} x
          %p
            Note that this property does not imply the dual
            %strong co-infinitely distributive
            property:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋀
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋀
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∨
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \vee (\bigwedge_i y_i) = \bigwedge_i (x\vee y_i)
          %p
            Instead this dual gives the lattice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Heyting+algebra"} co-Heyting
            structure where the co-implication or “subtraction” (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo \
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \backslash
            ) is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo \
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋀
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi v
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi x
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (u \backslash v) = \bigwedge_{u \leq v \vee x} x
          %p
            If a lattice has both properties, as in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completely+distributive+lattice"} completely distributive lattice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bi-Heyting+algebra"} bi-Heyting
            structure (both Heyting and co-Heyting), but the two exponentials are not necessarily equal.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋁
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi u
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi v
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (u \Rightarrow v) = \bigvee_{x \wedge u \leq v} x
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo \
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋀
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi v
                      %mo ∨
                      %mi x
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (u \backslash v) = \bigwedge_{u \leq v \vee x} x
          %blockquote
            %p Does it make sense to define “infinitely distributive property” for non-complete lattices? (Something like: “Whenever the join exists, it satisfies the infinite distributive law.”)
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#OppositeCategory Finite distributive lattices
          %p
            Since a finite distributive lattice is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completely+distributive+lattice"} completely distributive
            it is a bi-Heyting lattice, as shown above.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FinDistLat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinDistLat
            be the category of finite distributive lattices and lattice homomorphisms, and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FinPoset
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinPoset
            be the category of finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/posets"} posets
            and order-preserving functions. These are contravariantly equivalent, thanks to the presence of an
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ambimorphic+object"} ambimorphic object
          %p
            %strong Proposition.
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FinDistLat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinDistLat
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FinPoset
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinPoset
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi FinDistLat
                    %mi op
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi FinPoset
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  FinDistLat^{op} \simeq FinPoset.
          %p One direction of this equivalence is given by the hom-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi FinDistLat
                    %mi op
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi FinPoset
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [-,2] \;\colon\; FinDistLat^{op} \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} FinPoset
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            is the 2-element distributive lattice and for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi FinDistLat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in  FinDistLat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X,2]
            is the poset of distributive lattice morphisms from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi FinPoset
                    %mi op
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi FinDistLat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [-,2] \;\colon\; FinPoset^{op} \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} FinDistLat
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 = \{0,1\}
            is the 2-element poset with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \lt 1
            and for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} FinPoset
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \in \FinPoset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [Y,2]
            is the distributive lattice of poset morphisms from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            This
            %strong Birkhoff duality
            is (in one form or another) mentioned in many places; the formulation in terms of hom-functors may be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gavin+C.+Wraith"}> Gavin C. Wraith
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Using the generic interval
              %strong XXXIV
              4 (1993) pp.259-266. (
              %a{:href => "http://archive.numdam.org/ARCHIVE/CTGDC/CTGDC_1993__34_4/CTGDC_1993__34_4_259_0/CTGDC_1993__34_4_259_0.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p The functorial nature of the correspondence means that morphisms of finite posets (i.e. order-preserving maps) naturally correspond to morphisms of finite distributive lattices (i.e. order-preserving maps that also respect meet and join).
          %p
            It follows from Birkhoff’s representation theorem that every finite distributive lattice can be seen as a lattice of sets (i.e. sets with join and meet given by union and intersection) – in particular, sets whose elements are the join-irreducible elements of the lattice. Furthermore, a good intuition for why this duality holds is that either an element is generated as the join of existing elements, or it is join-irreducible. Hence given any existing poset, we can simply add all missing joins, and also a bottom (i.e. the nullary join). By general results (the adjoint functor theorem for posets) this suffices to ensure that all meets exist as well. This is analogous to the free colimit completion of a category, and indeed Birkhoff representation can be seen as a very special case of the Yoneda lemma as applied to (0,1)-category theory (i.e., order theory), since (0,1)-presheaves are functors into Bool rather than Set and hence correspond to
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lower+set"} lower set
          %p
            Birkhoff duality does not hold for infinite distributive lattices. However, in the general case of not-necessarily-finite distributive lattices there is a correspondence not to posets, but instead to a class of spaces known as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Priestley+spaces"} Priestley spaces
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+spaces"} coherent spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+locales"} coherent locales
            for the correspondence to distributive lattices. This is an instance of a general phenomena known as Stone-type duality.
          %h3#the_free_distributive_lattice The free distributive lattice
          %p Posets also give rise to a “free” distributive lattice, which is not the same as their Birkhoff dual. Instead, it is formed by the following procedure: First, take the poset of upsets with the reverse ordering (this is the free finite meet completion). Then form the distributive lattice of finitely generated downsets in that.
          %p
            In the case that one begins with a discrete poset (i.e., a set) then the number of elements in the resultant free distributive lattice is known as a Dedekind number, which also counts the number of monotone Boolean functions in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            variables. Dedekind numbers increase extremely rapidly, and there is no good known closed-form summation to compute them. The first ten (and only known) Dedekind numbers are (starting at
            = succeed "):" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 0
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#A000372"} OEIS A000372
          %h3#categorification Categorification
          %p
            Every distributive lattice, regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            (a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-category"} (0,1)-category
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+category"} coherent category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of the notion of distributive lattice. A different categorification is the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+category"} distributive category
          %h3#completion Completion
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completely+distributive+lattice"} completely distributive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+lattices"} algebraic lattices
            (the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames+of+opens"} frames of opens
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexandroff+locales"} Alexandroff locales
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subcategory"} reflective subcategory
            of that of all distributive lattices. The reflector is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+extension"} canonical extension
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
